  "slug": "evolution-history-state-formalism-a690",
  "summary": "Five preprints quantify what restricted control, readout, randomness and free operations can achieve in quantum state and observable manipulation.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is characterizing what a constrained set of operations can reach and at what cost. One preprint bounds the quantum-data copy cost of controlled amplitude amplification using no-signaling and distinguishability arguments, with quadratic scaling. Another studies reachability, geometry and preparation cost for multipartite states under differentiable collision-model control with partial-SWAP and Heisenberg exchange plus local phases. A third characterizes reachable effective observables of fixed-readout quantum neural networks under unitary, mixed-unitary and CPTP resources, with strict inclusions. A fourth shows eight labels suffice for interface choices in hybrid correlation generation with finite shared randomness. The fifth derives an exact deterministic conversion criterion for mixed states in quantum-state texture from a complete characterization of free operations. The new content is sharp, resource-specific boundaries rather than generic capability claims.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
